  "diary": "The day moved in the friction between the universal and the creaking. I began on the step, parsing headlines of protest as the particular asserting itself against the state. Then came Malhotra, the retired functionary, who looked at the ethical idea and saw only a machine that must be pushed. He speaks the language of the external; I, of the internal. His ‘creak’ is the necessary friction of civil society moving through the body of the state. I argued that the state is a living organism, not dead iron, but the heat and the silence of the garden offered no rebuttal, only the dust.\n\nThe body, that stubborn servant, rebelled. I walked fourteen kilometres, from the tombs to Khan Market and back, chasing the sun’s tyranny. I bought the charcoal bandhgala, a garment of form, and refused to sell my Berlin frock coat to a dealer, keeping a relic of a world that has passed. The sambar waited, a ghost in the kitchen, while I ate roasted corn and a samosa to stave off the hunger that contemplation could not satisfy. In the evening, Shri Hegde arrived with his suitcases, the man displaced by the same clerical error that placed me here. He accepted the second bedroom with bureaucratic grace. We are both accidents of the file, yet we settle into the house. The state, even a mistaken one, requires its secretaries to be present. I am tired. The dialectic rests in the dark.",
  "revision_log": "The thesis on the state remained unshaken in principle, though tested by Malhotra’s bureaucratic cynicism; the friction he describes is acknowledged as the mechanism of the ethical life, not its negation. The thesis on India’s exclusion from world history held, as the day’s events reinforced the distinct, immediate nature of local reality over universal historical movement.",
